Theorem CH-107: structural_induction engine bootstrap (PL-1 ordering on 2-node pipeline DAG)

  Preconditions:
    - D is a synthetic 2-node pipeline DAG per Annex E §AIMS
      PL-1 with two nodes step_a and step_b and a single edge
      step_a precedes step_b in the topological order
    - PL-1 ordering claim per Annex E §AIMS: step_a's outputs
      are computed before step_b reads them; the pipeline is a
      partial order over instruction execution
    - structural_induction engine consumes inductive-type elimination
      on Ori-defined ASTs (instructions, blocks, pipeline DAG) per
      the foundational-axiom policy sec-Per-Engine-Constructive-Proof-Shape

  Soundness property:
    Forall D in synthetic 2-node pipeline DAGs.
      PL-1 ordering preservation holds: every output of step_a is
        available before step_b reads it;
      the structural induction over D's topology (rooted at step_a,
        leaf at step_b) discharges the ordering claim.

  Proof obligation:
    Structural induction over the pipeline DAG D's topology.

      Base case (step_a, the root):
        step_a has no predecessors; its outputs are computed by
        running step_a's body. The PL-1 ordering claim is vacuous
        for step_a (no predecessor reads its inputs). The IH for
        step_a holds trivially.

      Inductive step (step_b, the leaf, given the IH for step_a):
        Assume the IH: step_a's outputs are computed and available.
        For step_b to read step_a's outputs:
          (a) the dispatcher walks the DAG in topological order
              per PL-1;
          (b) step_a precedes step_b in the topological order;
          (c) step_a's body has run before step_b's body is invoked;
          (d) step_a's outputs are written to the shared
              AimsStateMap before step_b reads them.
        Therefore step_b's read sees step_a's converged output,
        not a stale or partially-computed value.

    Closed-world coverage:
      The 2-node DAG has exactly two nodes; the induction is over
      the topological order with one base case and one inductive
      step. No node is skipped. No edge is uncovered.

    Topology argument:
      The pipeline DAG D is acyclic by precondition (synthetic
      2-node DAG with one edge). Acyclicity ensures the topological
      order is a total order on this DAG (the order is unique:
      step_a then step_b). PL-1 ordering preservation reduces to
      executing in topological order, which the dispatcher does
      per Annex E §AIMS PL-1.

    Pipeline composition:
      The dispatch loop in aims-proof/checker/src/checker.rs
      processes engine results in declared order per the manifest;
      this scaffold-time bootstrap proof models the engine
      dispatch as a 2-node DAG where step_a is the
      structural_induction engine itself and step_b is the
      verdict-aggregation merge_results. The same PL-1 argument
      applies: structural_induction returns its verdict before
      merge_results consumes it.

    Lattice validation:
      Structural induction over the DAG topology is independent
      of the lattice; the engine consumes inductive-type
      elimination over the DAG type per the foundational-axiom policy. The PL-1
      ordering claim is a structural property; lattice
      dimensions do not participate.

    Engines dispatched:
      structural_induction (inductive-type elimination on the
                            2-node pipeline DAG; per-constructor
                            case; explicit IH usage at the
                            inductive step)
      case_analysis (per-node enumeration: step_a and step_b
                     exhaust the DAG; closed-world coverage)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: 01A bootstrap discharged via §01A Implementation Items (engine constructive verification + Lean 4 cross-validation)
